WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.834 --> 00:00:03.336
HOST: All right,
guys, congratulations.

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:05.738
You both made it to round three
of the competition, where we

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:08.174
will send you to your
home forges for four days

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:11.811
to make this iconic weapon
from US military history.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:15.114
And that weapon is--

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.317
the Springfield 1905 bayonet.

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:20.586
We will be providing
a Springfield

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:23.623
1903 replica rifle to you.

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:30.263
Your bayonets must fit snugly
and securely on your rifle.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.497
 [bleep].

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.500
NARRATOR: Developed
in the early 1900s,

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.235
the M1905 Springfield
bayonet was

00:00:36.268 --> 00:00:39.338
used by American infantry
for nearly 70 years,

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:41.775
from World War I
through the Vietnam War.

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:43.309
These versatile
weapons were designed

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:46.046
with a mechanical device and the
pommel that attached securely

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:49.115
to the rifle, while also
featuring a comfortable handle,

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:51.951
making it a very effective
weapon in hand-to-hand combat.

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:56.122
Featuring a sharp single edge,
a long fuller, and a false edge,

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:59.091
this lightweight bayonet
could quickly deliver stabs

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:00.793
and slashes to an opponent.

00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:04.096
The bayonet is still used today
during military ceremonies

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:06.766
and can be seen in
several classic war films,

00:01:06.832 --> 00:01:09.435
like 1998's "The Thin Red Line."

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:11.571
HOST: All right, gentlemen, when
you guys build your bayonets,

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:13.306
I want you to follow
these parameters.

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:16.543
Your blade needs to be between
15 and 16 inches, with a fuller

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:20.880
and a false edge at the tip,
as well as a muzzle ring guard.

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.147
JOSH: I'm a little
nervous with it.

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:23.783
I've never done a guard
on a knife before,

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.017
let alone with a keyhole.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.485
And I know with the
bayonets, they usually

00:01:26.552 --> 00:01:28.287
have a place in the
back of the handle

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.922
where it locks into the rifle.

00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:31.390
There's a lot of firsts.

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:32.891
 All right,
gentlemen, good luck.

00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:33.860
We'll see you in four days.

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:34.660
 Good luck, man.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:35.428
 Yeah.

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:41.167 line:0%
BRAD: My main plan is to get
the whole blade forged out.

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:42.902
 I guess I could
grind it to shape.

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:45.105
But then it's not
forged in fire.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:51.811
BRAD: I have
everything forged out.

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:52.778
Fullers are in.

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:53.879
Rough dimensions are there.

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:55.014
I'm happy.

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:57.917
 So right now, I
have my groove cut.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:02:02.255
These are gonna be
the rest of my pommel.

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:06.392
They are going to go like so,
and then be welded like that.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:10.095
So I have to use my angle
grinder very, very, very

00:02:10.162 --> 00:02:13.932
carefully and scribe
this line in thick enough

00:02:13.999 --> 00:02:15.801
that it fits on those rails.

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:18.938
Line everything up.

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:20.506
Hey!

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:21.340
It works.

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.410
BRAD: I got my
scales all cut out.

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.412
They fit relatively nice.

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:28.247
Now I'm working
on getting my pin.

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:31.117
But that's gonna look
pretty cool when it's done.

00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:32.485
I have my knife all glued up.

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.954
Now that it's dry, I'm ready
to take the clamps off.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.223
And we'll start
profiling the handle.

00:02:38.257 --> 00:02:39.726
At the end process,
it's probably one

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:40.826
of the best things I've made.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.295 line:7%
I'm pretty proud of this.

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:44.697 line:0%
JOSH (VOICEOVER): So I'm back
in Lancaster, Ohio, at my home

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:45.565
forge.

00:02:45.631 --> 00:02:48.601
I've never made anything
this big before.

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:51.137
 All right, so got the
edges cleaned up fairly well.

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:53.439
Overall, my knife
is pretty straight.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:56.642
It's got some little wobbles in
there, but that's from forging.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.879
A little less than an 1/8 of an
inch to remove from this thing,

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:00.947
and we're good.

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:02.415
JOSH: I'm going to
clench my knife.

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:05.017
There's a lot of things that
can go wrong in this process.

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:06.186
 Let's do this.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:13.292
So I'm not hearing any
weird noises or any pings.

00:03:13.358 --> 00:03:15.528
So I think I'm in good shape.

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:18.965
The biggest challenge
of this build is getting

00:03:19.031 --> 00:03:21.667
that locking mechanism to work.

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:24.737
 So now, we're going
to test fit this.

00:03:27.773 --> 00:03:29.175
There she is.

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:31.577
I try to really pride
myself on my fit and finish,

00:03:31.643 --> 00:03:33.979
and I'm pretty
confident in my knife.

00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:35.314
I'm really happy with it.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.883
I wouldn't want to be on
the other end of this thing.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.284
Let's see what it does.

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:42.154
Yeah, this thing is sweet.

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:44.723
Let's see.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:45.692
[laughs]

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.029
This thing is sharp.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:51.563
Dude, that didn't
even make any noise.

00:03:51.597 --> 00:03:53.298
It just stabbed
right through it.

00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:56.502
 Bladesmiths, welcome
to the Kill Test.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:59.338
The first mission today
is to take your bayonet

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:00.840
and affix it to the
Springfield rifle

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:04.310
and deliver some killing blows
on this ballistics dummy.

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:07.513
Then I will dismantle
the blade from the rifle,

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.048
and let's see what
kind of lethal damage

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.183
it will do on its own.

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:11.083
Josh, you're up first.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:11.950
You ready?

00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:12.785
 As ready as I can be.

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:34.607
That's pretty bad ass.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.241
DOUG: All right, Josh, let's
talk about your bayonet here.

00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:38.410
When I affixed this
onto the rifle,

00:04:38.477 --> 00:04:41.380
it had a very nice
match with the railing

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:42.815
in there and your pin.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:43.950
It was very secure.

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.452
Now, your edge is very sharp.

00:04:46.518 --> 00:04:50.055
It definitely lacerates
deep into the bone.

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:52.258
On its own, the
handle construction

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:55.227
matches nicely on my hand.

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:57.730
Overall, sir, it will kill.

00:04:57.797 --> 00:04:58.531
 Awesome.

00:04:58.598 --> 00:04:59.231
Thank you.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:00.232
 All right, Brad, your turn.

00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:01.167
So are you ready for this?

00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:01.968
 Let's go for it.

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:19.051
Ooh.

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:22.221
DOUG: All right, Brad, first
up, the handle construction.

00:05:22.288 --> 00:05:24.357
It is a little bit
on the smaller side.

00:05:24.423 --> 00:05:26.092
But there are no sharp edges.

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:27.393
And the flair you
have here actually

00:05:27.426 --> 00:05:28.994
gives me good
retention, and I can

00:05:29.027 --> 00:05:30.396
get all the way to the guard.

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.298
At first, I was a little bit
worried about your cotter

00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:33.132
pin right here.

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:34.500
It looked flimsy.

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.735
But the minute I put it on,
it wasn't an issue at all.

00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:39.404
It stayed affixed to the
bayonet quite tightly.

00:05:39.471 --> 00:05:40.906
Your weapon is straight.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:44.610
Every cut was very deep
into this ballistics dummy.

00:05:44.676 --> 00:05:47.246
Overall, sir, your
weapon will kill.

00:05:47.313 --> 00:05:48.014
 Thank you, sir.

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.651
BEN ABBOTT: Bladesmiths,
welcome to the Strength Test,

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:55.821
the Steel Can Stab
and Barricade Chop.

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:58.290
This test is all about what
